WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.600
[a note, sung easily]

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.430
TAVI: So if I push the air out harder,
the note holds steadier?

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:14.287
MERC: Let's listen: the same note,
pushed harder and harder.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:20.245
Will the line settle, steadier than
before, or get louder and drift higher?

00:00:20.545 --> 00:00:22.545
[pause and predict]

00:00:22.895 --> 00:00:26.895
[the same note, pushed harder and harder]

00:00:27.095 --> 00:00:29.602
Louder, and higher.

00:00:29.685 --> 00:00:32.775
Pushing doesn't hold a note still:
it moves it.

00:00:33.175 --> 00:00:37.532
TAVI: But singers are told
to "support" a long note.

00:00:37.615 --> 00:00:41.420
If that doesn't mean pushing,
what keeps it steady?

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.820
[chime]

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.527
MERC: By the end,
you'll know what your diaphragm does,

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.627
how to breathe in low and start easily,

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.705
and you'll hold a comfortable
note for ten seconds.

00:00:56.305 --> 00:01:01.145
TAVI: From Lesson 3: how loud should
you sing when you practise matching?

00:01:01.495 --> 00:01:03.295
[think]

00:01:03.795 --> 00:01:05.992
MERC: At about the volume you talk at:

00:01:06.075 --> 00:01:09.450
a quiet note is just
as much the right note.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.955
TAVI: If I ease off,
does it come back down?

00:01:15.155 --> 00:01:19.532
MERC: How hard your breath pushes
on your vocal folds is its pressure.

00:01:19.615 --> 00:01:23.750
It mainly sets how loud the note is,
and it nudges the pitch.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:29.940
Listen, and watch the line:
the push rises, holds, then eases off.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:35.940
[a held note: the push rises,
holds, eases off]

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.637
TAVI: It came back.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:42.517
But Lesson 1 said loud
and high are different things.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:43.433
Aren't they?

00:01:43.517 --> 00:01:47.630
MERC: They are.
But in a voice, one push moves both.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:54.125
TAVI: Then where does that push come from?
Isn't it the diaphragm?

00:01:54.475 --> 00:01:58.527
MERC: Your diaphragm is
a dome-shaped muscle under your lungs.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.500
It tightens to breathe in.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:04.872
After a big breath,
you start a quiet note.

00:02:04.955 --> 00:02:08.505
Must your body push the air out,
or hold it back?

00:02:08.855 --> 00:02:10.855
[pause and predict]

00:02:11.205 --> 00:02:21.205
[Merc holds an A, 220 Hz, for ten seconds]

00:02:21.405 --> 00:02:25.525
Hold it back, with your breathing muscles,
never your throat.

00:02:25.725 --> 00:02:31.622
Full lungs spring back by themselves,
often harder than a quiet note needs.

00:02:31.705 --> 00:02:37.235
So at first your breathing-in muscles keep
working, holding some of that push back.

00:02:37.535 --> 00:02:42.807
As the air goes, your belly and rib
muscles take over the pushing.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.405
Your diaphragm can't push air out.

00:02:45.705 --> 00:02:51.010
TAVI: So full lungs push by themselves,
and at first I just hold some back.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:55.197
MERC: Yes. And a loud note
needs less holding back at first,

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.260
but more adding as the air runs low.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.247
TAVI: What about "support"?

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:03.940
Don't singers mean pushing hard,
or squeezing?

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:08.405
MERC: That's a common mix-up.
Pushing moves the note, as you heard.

00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:11.842
Squeezing your throat
makes a pressed sound,

00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:14.822
the sound people make
lifting something heavy,

00:03:14.905 --> 00:03:18.845
and a pressed sound needs
more air pressure, not less.

00:03:19.195 --> 00:03:24.242
"Support" is singers' word for how
they manage the breath as they sing.

00:03:24.325 --> 00:03:26.370
There's no one agreed meaning.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:34.147
A steady note needs steady air, not
more of it: breathe in low, start easily,

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.695
and let the air flow out unforced.

00:03:39.595 --> 00:03:41.095
TAVI: How should I breathe in?

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.052
MERC: Start tall but easy.

00:03:43.135 --> 00:03:46.052
If you're standing,
keep your knees unlocked.

00:03:46.135 --> 00:03:49.115
Let your jaw, tongue and neck go loose.

00:03:49.465 --> 00:03:54.832
Then breathe in quietly, and let
the breath drop low, shoulders still,

00:03:54.915 --> 00:03:57.105
taking as much as feels easy.

00:04:00.005 --> 00:04:01.805
TAVI: And how should the note start?

00:04:02.115 --> 00:04:08.145
MERC: How a note starts is its onset.
A voice has three main ways. Just listen.

00:04:08.445 --> 00:04:13.670
Air first: a puff of breath,
like a soft "h", then the sound.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.620
[a start: air first]

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:21.387
A catch: the vocal folds are pressed shut,
and the note starts as they open,

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:22.750
a little like a cough.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.700
[a start: a catch]

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.630
Or together: the folds meet
the air just as it starts to flow.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.580
[a start: together]

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:37.345
Back to back now: listen for the puff,
then the click, then neither.

00:04:37.695 --> 00:04:42.895
[the three starts, back to back]

00:04:43.395 --> 00:04:48.292
For a long, easy note,
many singing teachers aim for the third,

00:04:48.375 --> 00:04:50.327
the easy start.

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:56.200
It's often found by beginning with a soft
"h", then keeping it only in your mind.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:03.185
TAVI: What does a steady note look like?
A perfectly straight line?

00:05:03.585 --> 00:05:06.200
MERC: Tavi, hold an A for ten seconds.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:16.600
[Tavi holds an A, 440 Hz, for ten seconds]

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:21.000
It scooped, and it waves.
Is that still steady?

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.047
MERC: It is.

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.892
Sung notes are rarely perfectly still:

00:05:24.975 --> 00:05:28.065
they can begin with a short slide,
a scoop.

00:05:28.415 --> 00:05:32.232
And steady doesn't mean dead straight:
a small vibrato,

00:05:32.315 --> 00:05:35.965
a regular wave in the pitch,
can sit inside the band.

00:05:36.265 --> 00:05:41.667
Steady means the line settles into
the band and stays, for a comfortable,

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:44.940
fixed length, never "as long as you can".

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.845
TAVI: Then is it steady air
that keeps the note there?

00:05:49.345 --> 00:05:53.212
MERC: No. Muscles in your
voice box set the note.

00:05:53.295 --> 00:05:58.500
A steady note needs steady air too,
because a change in the air moves it.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:05.605
Your turn: one comfortable note,
held for ten seconds.

00:06:05.805 --> 00:06:11.820
Lesson 3's care lines still apply: stop
if your voice feels scratchy or hoarse.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:16.510
Sore, hoarse or unwell today?
Just listen along.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:22.022
Choose listening if anything hurts
or singing has felt uncomfortable,

00:06:22.105 --> 00:06:26.127
if your voice feels
unusually hoarse or tired,

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:31.132
if notes you usually reach have
suddenly gone, if you're ill,

00:06:31.215 --> 00:06:34.715
or if talking or singing
feels like hard work today.

00:06:35.065 --> 00:06:38.665
Listening along?
Rest your voice and watch the ring.

00:06:39.015 --> 00:06:42.895
Otherwise, sing my A,
or Tavi's, an octave up.

00:06:43.095 --> 00:06:46.962
Listen to our note.
Breathe in, low and quiet.

00:06:47.045 --> 00:06:50.965
Then sing straight away,
easily, at about speaking volume.

00:06:51.165 --> 00:06:55.500
Hold the note until the ring closes,
or stop sooner if you need to.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:59.085
Stop immediately if
anything feels uncomfortable.

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:01.685
[Merc and Tavi sing their A together]

00:07:01.785 --> 00:07:02.695
MERC: Breathe in.

00:07:03.795 --> 00:07:04.628
[tick: start]

00:07:04.712 --> 00:07:13.795
[your ten seconds]

00:07:13.878 --> 00:07:14.712
[tick: stop]

00:07:14.795 --> 00:07:19.790
How did that feel? Easy, workable,
effortful, or uncomfortable?

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:21.940
[think]

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:27.375
Easy or workable?
Rest for a few easy breaths.

00:07:27.725 --> 00:07:31.950
Effortful? Keep it gentle:
rest before you sing again.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:34.977
Uncomfortable? Thanks for saying so.

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:38.055
That's enough singing for today:
rest your voice.

00:07:38.405 --> 00:07:39.907
Whatever you chose:

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:44.157
if hoarseness or discomfort
lasts more than a couple of weeks,

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:50.307
see a doctor (a GP or an ear,
nose and throat specialist).

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:54.217
If it's very painful,
or breathing or swallowing is hard,

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:55.930
get help straight away.

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:58.263
Quick check.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:01.085
What is your diaphragm's main job?

00:08:01.435 --> 00:08:03.235
[think]

00:08:03.535 --> 00:08:04.500
Breathing in.

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:09.705
For a long, easy note, which start
do many singing teachers aim for?

00:08:10.055 --> 00:08:11.855
[think]

00:08:12.155 --> 00:08:15.810
Air and sound starting together:
the easy start.

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:20.405
TAVI: What does a steady long
note look like on the pitch line?

00:08:20.755 --> 00:08:22.555
[think]

00:08:23.155 --> 00:08:26.110
MERC: It settles into
the band and stays there.

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:29.417
Practise it.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:34.987
Voice Mirror has you glide up and down,
hold any comfortable note for six seconds,

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.660
then sing back five notes it plays.

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:43.950
Keep the glides comfortable.
Sore, hoarse or unwell today?

00:08:44.033 --> 00:08:50.210
Just listen along.
Free, no account: mercurypitch.com/mirror.

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:55.621
Felt uncomfortable,
or listened along today? Rest today.

00:08:55.704 --> 00:09:01.186
Voice Mirror will be here when
your voice feels comfortable:

00:09:01.269 --> 00:09:03.495
mercurypitch.com/mirror.

00:09:06.395 --> 00:09:07.375
Next time.

00:09:07.725 --> 00:09:11.725
[Merc sings the first line of a tune]

00:09:11.925 --> 00:09:16.400
I move every single note
of "Twinkle, Twinkle" up.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.482
TAVI: Why can you start "Twinkle, Twinkle"

00:09:19.565 --> 00:09:23.140
on any note and it is still the same tune?

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:29.457
[chime]
